 Move a task out of `human` back into `work`. With no `--to`, it re-enters the
 step it was parked in. `--to STEP` jumps to a sibling step in the same workflow;
-`--to done|cancel|human` relocates to a terminal/park status instead.
+`--to EXIT` relocates to a terminal/park status instead — but only one the
+task's workflow declares as an exit (its external-operations register, shown as
+`exits` by `autosk workflow show`). A workflow that declares no register keeps
+the `done|cancel|human` union. A move the workflow declines exits 2 — a target
+that names nothing, an exit the register does not declare, or a relocation the
+graph's own rules refuse. A fault inside the workflow's hooks or the transport
+is an internal error and exits 1.
 
 ```bash
 autosk resume ask-bea935                # back to the parked step
 autosk resume ask-bea935 --to dev       # jump to the dev step
-autosk resume ask-bea935 --to cancel    # park-to-terminal
+autosk resume ask-bea935 --to cancel    # park-to-terminal, where declared
 ```
 
 Prints the updated task (honors `--json` / `--quiet`).
